<Base slug="goals/" title="Goals, non-goals and success" description="What The Cousin must achieve first, what it deliberately won’t do yet, and how we’ll know it works.">
  <header class="wrap phead">
    <p class="chapter">02 · Goals, non-goals and success</p>
    <h1 class="h1" data-split="lines" data-intro>Prove that friends actually go out on its ideas.</h1>
    <p class="lede muted" data-reveal="up" data-intro data-delay="0.3">One city, one evening at a time, for the founder and 3 to 5 friends first. Everything else waits until that works.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">First users</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The founder plus 3 to 5 friends in Chennai, aged roughly 18 to 30. The first situation to get right is the vaguest one: <b>“I’m bored, surprise me.”</b> If the app can turn that into an evening people enjoy, the specific requests are easier.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Success in month one</p>
    <div class="signals">
      <div><b>They go</b><p>Ideas turn into real outings, not just saved lists.</p></div>
      <div><b>“I’d never have found that”</b><p>Some outings are new to the person, and they loved them.</p></div>
      <div><b>Fewer back-and-forths</b><p>The first reply needs less fixing each week.</p></div>
      <div><b>They go back</b><p>For clubs and communities, the second visit matters more than the first.</p></div>
      <p class="muted small">We measure outings, not screen time. Minutes in the app are not a goal.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Goals</p>
    <ol class="plain">
      <li><b>Understand any request</b> about going out, from “I’m bored” to a two-stop plan with a deadline.</li>
      <li><b>Never break a stated limit.</b> Budget, diet, group size and deadlines always hold.</li>
      <li><b>Never invent a fact.</b> Every name, price and time shown comes from data, printed by code.</li>
      <li><b>Follow changes of mind</b> in one step: “only ₹500”, “we’re 10 now”, “scrap it”.</li>
      <li><b>Say no clearly</b> to unsafe or out-of-scope asks, and offer the nearest safe thing.</li>
      <li><b>Get better with use:</b> remember what worked, rest places just visited, notice when taste changes.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Not yet</p>
    <div class="not">
      <p class="muted">Each of these was discussed and deliberately parked. The design leaves room for them.</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><b>Intercity rides and multi-day trips.</b> A Chennai to Bangalore ride or a 5-day visitor plan gets “not yet, here’s a single evening”.</li>
        <li><b>Bookings and payments.</b> Later, and anything that costs money always needs the user’s yes.</li>
        <li><b>Voice.</b> The end goal is speak, hear a reply, see the plan on a map. The core stays the same; voice wraps it.</li>
        <li><b>Other cities and languages.</b> English first, Chennai first.</li>
        <li><b>Paid placement.</b> If it ever happens, it is labelled and kept out of the normal ranking.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="door">
    <div class="wrap row airy">
      <p class="chapter">Principles</p>
      <ol class="principles">
        <li><span>The AI is the voice, not the brain.</span> It listens and explains. Code chooses, checks and remembers.</li>
        <li><span>Limits before taste.</span> Hard rules remove options first; only then does scoring rank what’s left.</li>
        <li><span>At most one question per reply.</span> Guess sensibly, say the guess out loud, ask only when a wrong guess is costly.</li>
        <li><span>A habit, not an addiction.</span> The reward happens out in the city, once or twice a week.</li>
        <li><span>Every model earns its place.</span> A trained model replaces a rule only after beating it on real people.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

